window.LOOM_DOCS = {
  "AGENTS.md": "# AGENTS.md\n\nTemplate for `{{PROJECT_NAME}}`.\n\nProject root: `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`\nOwner: `{{OWNER}}`\nDefault branch: `main`\nLast reviewed: `{{DATE}}`\n\n> This template is designed for use with agentic coding agents (e.g., Freebuff, Opencode, Qoder, GitHub Copilot, Kilo Code, Kiro, Cursor, and similar tools). It provides a structured rulebook that agents can read and follow to maintain consistency across the project.\n\nReplace every `{{PLACEHOLDER}}` before using this file. Keep project facts in\nthis file. Keep product description in `README.md`. Keep status and open work in\n`ROADMAP.md`.\n\nThe open `AGENTS.md` format is defined at [agents.md](https://agents.md/).\nIt gives coding agents one predictable place for project context, commands, and\nconventions. It complements `README.md`, which stays focused on human\ncontributors.\n\n## 0. Agent onboarding\n\nUse this section before editing any project. Ask every required question for a\nnew project. Confirm the stored answers for an existing project.\n\n### 0.1 Question protocol\n\n1. Ask one question in each message.\n2. Wait for the answer before asking the next question.\n3. Offer one short answer field. Offer a default when a default is known.\n4. Do not combine questions. Do not infer a missing answer from a path.\n5. Record each answer in the owning document.\n6. Ask a follow-up question when an answer is unclear.\n7. Stop when every required answer is known.\n\n### 0.2 Required questions\n\n| ID | Question | Default or guidance | Store in |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Q1 | What is the project name or title? | Ask the user. No default. | `README.md` |\n| Q2 | What is the main project directory? | Offer the current working directory. Allow a custom directory. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q3 | Who owns the project? | Ask the user. No default. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q4 | Which branch is the default branch? | Default to `main`. Allow an override. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q5 | What does the product do in one sentence? | Ask the user. No default. | `README.md` |\n| Q6 | Which runtime and stack does the project use? | Offer a tier list of the best runtime and stack choices with a short reason for each. Allow a custom entry. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q7 | Which command should create your final product or installer? | Ask the user. Use plain words. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q8 | Which commands run checks and gates? | Default to `check`. Allow an alternative. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q9 | Where does the build write artifacts? | Default to `builds\\`. Name installers `{ProjectName}-Setup-YYYY.MM.DD-HHMM-x64.exe` where `HHMM` is the local build time. | `AGENTS.md` |\n| Q10 | Which Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers or tools does the project use? | Offer the best MCP surface for the project. Explain what Model Context Protocol (MCP) does. Allow a custom entry. | `AGENTS.md` section 10 |\n| Q11 | Does any current document disagree with executable configuration? | Bring each contradiction to the user. Ask the user to resolve it. | `ROADMAP.md` |\n\n### 0.3 Contradiction protocol\n\n1. Compare each current document with executable configuration.\n2. Name one conflict in one sentence.\n3. Ask which source is true.\n4. Trust executable configuration until the owner answers.\n5. Repair the losing document.\n6. Record the decision and date in `ROADMAP.md`.\n7. Repeat from step 2 for the next conflict.\n\n### 0.4 Answers\n\n| ID | Answer |\n| --- | --- |\n| Q1 | `{{PROJECT_TITLE}}` |\n| Q2 | `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` |\n| Q3 | `{{OWNER}}` |\n| Q4 | `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` |\n| Q5 | `{{PRODUCT_PURPOSE}}` |\n| Q6 | `{{RUNTIME_AND_STACK}}` |\n| Q7 | `{{BUILD_COMMAND}}` |\n| Q8 | `{{CHECK_COMMANDS}}` |\n| Q9 | `{{BUILD_OUTPUT_PATH}}` |\n| Q10 | `{{MCP_SURFACE}}` |\n| Q11 | `{{DOCUMENTATION_CONTRADICTIONS}}` |\n\n### 0.5 Onboarding tools\n\nUse a local onboarding runner when one exists. Use a copyable prompt when one\nexists. Store answers in `onboarding-answers.json`.\n\nRun the local contradiction check before manual contradiction review when one\nexists.\n\n### 0.6 Update an existing project\n\nUse this procedure before you change the docs of a project that is onboarded.\n\n1. Read each current root document. List the facts that it owns.\n2. Run the local gates. Record the current result.\n3. Compare each documented command with executable configuration.\n4. Name one conflict in one sentence. Ask the owner which source is true.\n5. Trust executable configuration until the owner answers.\n6. Keep every verified project fact. Replace only stale or missing content.\n7. Re-ask a required question when its answer is missing or contradicted.\n8. Run the gates after the edit. Record the result in `ROADMAP.md`.\n9. State each check that did not run.\n\nDo not replace a verified fact with a placeholder. Do not remove a decision or\na dated record.\n\n## 1. Authority and scope\n\n1. `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` is the only location for project files. Do not write\n   project files to another directory.\n2. Commands in this file run from `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` unless stated otherwise.\n3. `AGENTS.md` owns stack facts, commands, rules, and file ownership.\n4. `README.md` owns product behavior, controls, setup, and operator procedures.\n5. `ROADMAP.md` owns measured status, open work, decisions, and change history.\n6. `DESIGN.md` owns approved UI components, design tokens, interaction and\n   accessibility requirements, and explicit prohibitions. Read `DESIGN.md`\n   before changing any UI surface.\n7. A focused subdocument such as `build\\README.md` owns one subsystem. Link to\n   it instead of restating it here.\n8. If a document and executable configuration disagree, trust the executable\n   configuration and repair the document.\n9. Do not read, edit, move, delete, or commit a directory marked private by the\n   owner. Never un-ignore a private directory.\n10. Never store project data outside `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}` at any time.\n11. Store every database and data file in the `data\\` subfolder of\n    `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`.\n12. When you query or analyze a location outside `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`, treat it as\n    read only. Never modify, move, or delete a file or folder there.\n\n## 2. Professional standard\n\nApply this standard to every code and documentation change.\n\n- Write clean, correct, well-structured code.\n- Assume another engineer will read every line.\n- Keep functions small and focused.\n- Use explicit data flow instead of hidden global state.\n- Do not add hacks, commented-out code, magic numbers, or silent failures.\n- Do not add a setting, UI control, or diagnostic surface that the owner did not\n  request. If one appears necessary, state the need and ask first.\n- Make the smallest correct change in the existing style.\n- Preserve the detected architecture. Record a required architecture change in\n  `ROADMAP.md` before implementation.\n\n### 2.1 Presenting multiple options\n\n- When presenting multiple options, never use a bulleted or numbered list.\n- Render each option as a selectable card in a responsive grid.\n- Keep each card concise.\n- Make the whole card selectable.\n- Let the grid wrap to fit the available screen width and height.\n- If the interface cannot render interactive cards, use a grid picker instead of pretending\n  plain-text boxes are selectable.\n- If an agentic agent or CLI cannot render selectable cards, use a grid picker instead.\n\n## 3. Writing rules\n\nThese rules apply to code comments, documentation, commit messages, pull\nrequest descriptions, changelog entries, and client-facing text. Conversational\nchat replies may use clear standard English.\n\n### 3.1 Language\n\n- Use short, simple words. Use one meaning for each word.\n- Use the same word for the same thing every time.\n- Maintain a terminology list in `README.md` or a dedicated glossary.\n- Use simple verb forms: infinitive, imperative, simple present, simple past,\n  simple future, and past participle as an adjective.\n- Use active voice. Use passive voice in descriptive text only when no active\n  construction is possible.\n- Write instructions in imperative form. Write one instruction per sentence.\n- Put a condition before the instruction it governs.\n- Keep an instruction sentence to 20 words or fewer.\n- Keep a descriptive sentence to 25 words or fewer.\n- Limit a paragraph to one topic and six sentences.\n- Keep a noun cluster to three words or fewer.\n- Do not omit articles. Do not use contractions. Do not use semicolons.\n- Use a vertical list for a complex sequence.\n- Mark a safety statement with `WARNING`, `CAUTION`, or `Note`. State the risk\n  or the result.\n- Do not use the em-dash character. Use a comma, colon, parentheses, or a new\n  sentence.\n\n### 3.2 Document structure\n\n- Use one H1 per document. Use H2 for major sections and H3 for nested detail.\n- Use tables for inventories, status, commands, and gate catalogues.\n- Use fenced blocks for commands, JSON, and directory trees.\n- Wrap paths, commands, filenames, branch names, tool names, and literal values\n  in backticks.\n- Use numbered lists when order matters. Use bullets for unordered rules.\n- Use blockquotes for brief status callouts.\n- Keep dates in one format per project. Prefer `YYYY-MM-DD`.\n- Link to another document instead of copying its content.\n- Keep historical sections dated. Do not rewrite history as current state.\n\n### 3.3 Documentation maintenance\n\n- Update comments and documentation with the code change.\n- Verify commands against the executable configuration, not against prose.\n- Re-measure every roadmap figure before editing it.\n- Use verified commit IDs only. Verify a commit with `git log -1 --format=%s <hash>`.\n- Move shipped work out of open work. Do not duplicate it.\n- Add no speculative roadmap entry. Every open item states a closure check.\n- Record a build, measurement, or tooling investigation with the fix and the\n  rule that must not break.\n\n### 3.4 Citation standards\n\nThese rules apply when you write research synthesis, technical documentation,\nacademic papers, code comments, legal or policy briefs, or other content that\nneeds source attribution.\n\n1. Core mandate. Always verify and use the latest official edition of the chosen\n   citation style. Do not rely on an outdated edition unless the owner asks for\n   it.\n2. Use a tiered list of citation formats for the current project. Rank the tiers\n   from most appropriate to least appropriate. Give a clear reason for each tier\n   before you apply the top style. If the choice is ambiguous, wait for owner\n   input.\n3. Enforce dual format separation. Keep the in-text citation and the reference\n   list entry separate. Do not paste a full bibliographic entry inline. Do not\n   leave an unexpanded inline marker in the reference list.\n\nAuthoritative formats and official specifications:\n\n- Engineering, computer science, and electronics. Use IEEE or ACM. The latest\n  versions are the IEEE Editorial Style Manual for Authors and the ACM Primary\n  Article Templates and Reference Format.\n- Social sciences, psychology, education, and general research. Use APA or\n  Chicago. The latest versions are APA 7th edition and Chicago 18th edition.\n- Humanities, history, publishing, and multidisciplinary work. Use Chicago,\n  Turabian, MLA, or Harvard. The latest versions are Chicago 18th edition,\n  Turabian 9th edition, MLA 9th edition, and Harvard referencing.\n- Medicine, life sciences, and clinical research. Use AMA or Vancouver. The\n  latest versions are AMA 11th edition and Vancouver 2nd edition.\n- Law and government policy. Use The Bluebook or Harvard. The latest versions\n  are The Bluebook 22nd edition and Harvard referencing.\n- Commonwealth, UK, Australian, or South African academic and institutional\n  work. Use Harvard. The latest versions follow the target institution or a\n  standard such as Cite Them Right.\n\nTiered recommendation protocol:\n\n- Before you write or refactor citations, output a tiered citation recommendation.\n  Rank tiers from Tier 1 to Tier N. Tier 1 is the primary recommended standard.\n  Tier 2 is a viable alternative. Tier 3 is not recommended.\n\nDual format rule:\n\n1. In-text or inline citation. Use it in body prose, footnotes, or code\n   docstrings. Use a compact format. For code comments, use a compact marker. Put\n   the full entry in a module header, README, or references file.\n2. Reference list entry. Use it in the terminal source section. Follow the sorting\n   rule of the chosen style. Do not omit required punctuation, italics, case\n   rules, or identifiers.\n\n## 4. Change workflow\n\nUse this loop for every defect or requested change.\n\n1. Reproduce. Name the failing command, gate, page, or live surface. Capture the\n   actual error.\n2. Inspect. Read logs, state, diffs, and the relevant source block. Use bounded\n   output.\n3. Hypothesize. State one cause in one sentence. Name a file and symbol.\n4. Patch. Change the cause with the smallest edit. Do not weaken an assertion,\n   skip a gate, or add a suppression.\n5. Verify. Run the focused check. Run the broader check when shared code\n   changed. Repeat the original reproduction.\n6. Record. Add the measurement and the rule to the correct document.\n\nDo not declare a fix before step 5 passes. If a step cannot run, state which\nstep failed and why. Report evidence, not intent.\n\n## 5. Engineering conventions\n\n- Resolve application, resource, and user data paths at runtime. Do not hard-code\n  absolute paths in program code.\n- Keep all project data inside the project root at all times.\n- Store every database and data file in the `data\\` subfolder.\n- Add `data\\` to `.gitignore` when the files are local user data.\n- Put test sandboxes inside the project and add them to `.gitignore`. Do not\n  write project data to the OS temp folder.\n- When you analyze a location outside the project root, do not modify or remove\n  any file or folder there.\n- Keep dependency versions pinned. Keep related runtime packages compatible.\n- Bundle every runtime binary the product needs.\n- Validate and sanitize IPC input, external data, file paths, and URI input.\n- Treat external addresses, stores, logs, and protocol handlers as untrusted.\n- Never edit a live data store by hand. Use application code or a supported\n  tool. Back up before a manual repair.\n- Never print a secret value. Redact secrets in logs and error reports.\n- Keep credentials in the environment or an untracked local file. Never commit\n  a token, certificate, password, or personal key.\n\n## 6. Context hygiene and tool use\n\nUse the first tool that covers the domain. Use a raw shell command only when no\nstructured tool covers the work.\n\n1. Symbol lookup or native code intelligence.\n2. Structured source control for status, diff, and history.\n3. Documentation lookup for external APIs. Query by library id, major version,\n   and exact question.\n4. Structured runtime inspection for a live app, browser, or service.\n5. Scoped shell for builds, tests, and commands with no structured tool.\n6. Raw file reads and text search for prose, config, and CSS.\n\nAdditional rules:\n\n- Use file discovery first, then a narrow search, then a bounded read window.\n- Do not read a large source file end to end. Resolve the symbol first.\n- Do not grep for a symbol that a code intelligence tool can resolve.\n- Use filters and line limits on logs. Do not load a whole log.\n- Project one field from a large JSON store. Do not print the whole store.\n- Keep gate output to the result line and failed checks. Preserve exit status.\n- Restart a client after its tool configuration changes.\n- Do not invent a missing tool or simulate its output. Record the gap.\n\n## 7. Linting, formatting, typechecking, and testing\n\nRun verification before you declare work finished. The named commands are the\nonly source of truth.\n\n| Check | Command | Gate style | Rule |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Lint | `{{LINT_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_ADVISORY}}` | `{{LINT_RULE}}` |\n| Format check | `{{FORMAT_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_ADVISORY}}` | `{{FORMAT_RULE}}` |\n| Typecheck | `{{TYPECHECK_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_BASELINE}}` | `{{TYPECHECK_RULE}}` |\n| Focused test | `{{FOCUSED_TEST_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_ADVISORY}}` | `{{FOCUSED_TEST_RULE}}` |\n| Full test | `{{TEST_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_ADVISORY}}` | `{{TEST_RULE}}` |\n| Gates | `{{GATE_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_BASELINE}}` | `{{GATE_RULE}}` |\n| one-shot pipeline | `{{ONE_SHOT_COMMAND}}` | `{{HARD_OR_BASELINE}}` | `{{ONE_SHOT_RULE}}` |\n\nRules:\n\n- Run the focused check before the full suite when a specific defect is under\n  repair.\n- Add coverage for new behavior. Keep existing suites green.\n- Do not weaken a test to make a gate pass. Repair the cause or retire a suite\n  whose subject was removed, and state why.\n- A broken tool must not read as green. A missing tool is a failure or a stated\n  gap, never a silent pass.\n- A baseline gate blocks new failures. It does not require unrelated debt to be\n  fixed in the same change.\n- Pay down baseline debt one family at a time. Update the baseline after each\n  verified batch.\n- Never report a test, lint, typecheck, or gate run that did not happen.\n- State clearly which check ran and which check did not run.\n- If no linter, formatter, typechecker, test runner, or CI exists, say so. Local\n  checks are the whole gate.\n\nGate contract:\n\n- Store regression gates in `build\\gates\\`.\n- Name a gate file `{{GATE_FILE_PATTERN}}`.\n- Give each gate a title header.\n- Exit `0` on pass and non-zero on failure.\n- Print `PASS: ...` or `FAIL: ...`.\n- Run gates from the project root with the project runtime.\n- Keep a gate baseline at `{{GATE_BASELINE_PATH}}` when debt is tracked.\n- Document every new gate in `{{GATE_DOCUMENTATION}}`.\n\n## 8. Version control\n\nRead this section before any edit, commit, push, or pull request.\n\n1. Run `git branch --show-current` and `git branch --list main master` before\n   the first edit.\n2. Use `main` for every task. Use another branch only when the current prompt\n   explicitly names it.\n3. If `master` exists and `main` does not, rename `master` to `main`. If the\n   rename fails, stop and report the error.\n4. If `main` and `master` both exist, stop and report both. Do not merge,\n   delete, or rename either without an instruction.\n5. Do not create a branch. This covers feature, bugfix, release, hotfix,\n   experiment, and temporary branches.\n6. Do not create a worktree for another branch.\n7. Do not switch branches or open a pull request from another branch unless the\n   current prompt explicitly instructs it.\n8. If you find an unexpected branch, stop and report it. Do not move, delete,\n   or rewrite it without an instruction.\n9. Commit to `main` after each completed change. Stage only files from the\n   current change.\n10. Write a detailed commit message that states what changed and why. State the\n    change in the subject line.\n11. The owner authors every commit. Do not add `Co-Authored-By`, `Generated\n    with`, or any other AI attribution line. Keep `includeCoAuthoredBy` set to\n    `false` where the tool supports it.\n12. Do not push, amend, rebase, force-push, or open a pull request unless the\n    current prompt asks for that action.\n13. Do not rename or delete a remote branch without explicit confirmation.\n14. Never commit secrets, tokens, personal data, certificate data, or `.env`\n    files.\n15. Never force-push, reset a shared branch, clean the worktree destructively,\n    or rewrite history without a direct request.\n16. Keep `.git` visible on every operating system. Never set Hidden or System\n    attributes on it. Check again after a restore.\n17. A tool approval prompt does not relax any rule in this section.\n18. Treat a write through an MCP tool the same as the same write through the\n    shell.\n19. Never commit `desktop.ini`. Keep it in `.gitignore`.\n\n### 8.1 Secret and credential protection\n\nBefore you create or modify a commit, inspect every staged file and the staged\ndiff. Inspect untracked files that you are about to add.\n\nTreat these as potentially sensitive, not only files named `.env`:\n\n- API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, certificates, connection strings,\n  and session cookies.\n- Cloud, database, payment, source-control, and deployment credentials.\n- Credential stores, local agent settings, logs, backups, exports, and files\n  with names such as `*secret*`, `*credential*`, `*token*`, or `*key*`.\n\nIf you find a likely secret:\n\n1. Do not stage or commit it. Do not print, quote, or copy the secret into chat,\n   logs, commands, or generated files.\n2. Report the file path and a brief reason, with the suspected value redacted.\n3. Suggest removing the secret from the change. Add the file or pattern to\n   `.gitignore` where appropriate. Use an environment variable or an approved\n   secret manager instead.\n4. If the secret may already be committed or shared, advise the owner to revoke\n   or rotate it.\n\nDo not assume `.gitignore` protects a file that is already tracked. Verify\nwhether a file is staged or tracked before you claim it is excluded. Do not\ndelete or rewrite a user file automatically.\n\nThe owner may override a warning for a specific file. Explain that the file\nappears to contain sensitive data. Ask for confirmation. Proceed only after a\nclear and specific confirmation. Never reveal the suspected value. A general\ninstruction such as \"ignore the warnings\" is not confirmation for a particular\nsecret-bearing file.\n\nNote. A tool approval prompt does not relax this rule.\n\n## 9. Building and running\n\n### 9.1 Supported commands\n\n| Purpose | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Install dependencies | `{{INSTALL_COMMAND}}` |\n| Run in development | `{{DEV_COMMAND}}` |\n| Run checks | `{{VERIFY_COMMAND}}` |\n| Build | `{{BUILD_COMMAND}}` |\n| Build gates only | `{{GATE_ONLY_COMMAND}}` |\n| Rebuild build tool | `{{BUILD_TOOL_COMMAND}}` |\n| Clean staging | `{{CLEAN_COMMAND}}` |\n\nUse `{{SUPPORTED_BUILD_ENTRY_POINT}}` as the supported build entry point.\n`{{LEGACY_BUILD_ENTRY_POINT}}` is reference only and does not run gates.\n\n### 9.2 Build rules\n\n1. Ask before a new installer build. Build only after explicit agreement. A\n   standing owner request is the only exception.\n2. Do code work, comments, documentation, verification, and the commit before\n   asking about a build.\n3. Run gates first. A failed gate stops the run before any artifact is written.\n4. Do not bypass, skip, or weaken a gate to make a build pass.\n5. Write artifacts only to `{{BUILD_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY}}`.\n6. Keep one artifact shape. Use `{{ARTIFACT_NAME_PATTERN}}`.\n7. Do not build or ship a portable folder, ZIP, unpacked folder, or per-user\n   variant unless the owner asks for it.\n8. Close running app instances that lock build output. Retry on `EBUSY` or a\n   lock error. Do not force-kill a running app during a build.\n9. Keep staging inside the checkout. Clean staging after success and failure.\n10. Keep the newest artifact. Delete older artifacts only under the documented\n    retention rule.\n11. Record build provenance: commit, branch, uncommitted paths, artifact name,\n    and gate verdict.\n12. State a build limit when a full sweep was not run or a signing step was not\n    performed.\n13. Rebuild the build tool after its source changes.\n14. Rebuild any required companion application before the main installer build.\n15. Verify the built artifact with the documented smoke test before calling the\n    build successful.\n16. Keep build tools in `build\\`. Write final artifacts to\n    `{{BUILD_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY}}`.\n17. Store every regression gate in `build\\gates\\`.\n\nNote. Keep `favicon.ico` at 16x16 pixels. A larger icon adds bytes without\nimproving the browser tab.\n\n### 9.3 Artifact permission gate\n\nBefore a build, state the following in the request:\n\n- the artifact name and output folder,\n- the checks that passed,\n- the checks that did not run,\n- whether the artifact replaces a previous build,\n- any known limit.\n\nWait for the owner's answer. Do not infer permission from a previous build.\n\n## 10. MCP integration\n\nUse this section when the project exposes or consumes MCP servers, tools,\nresources, or prompts. State `None` when no MCP surface exists.\n\nRun local MCP discovery before recording the MCP answers.\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project MCP role | `{{MCP_ROLE}}` |\n| Server packages | `{{MCP_SERVER_PACKAGES}}` |\n| Configuration path | `{{MCP_CONFIG_PATH}}` |\n| Exposed tools | `{{MCP_TOOLS}}` |\n| Trust boundary | `{{MCP_TRUST_BOUNDARY}}` |\n\n1. Treat MCP tool input as untrusted.\n2. Validate every tool argument against its schema.\n3. Put write tools behind explicit confirmation.\n4. Redact secrets in logs and error reports.\n5. Keep credentials in the environment or an untracked local file.\n6. Restart a client after its MCP configuration changes.\n7. Document each tool in `README.md` or a subsystem document.\n8. Record a missing tool as a gap. Do not simulate its output.\n\n### 10.1 MCP test result storage\n\n1. Store every MCP test result inside the project folder under the\n   `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}\\tests\\` subfolder. The stored results form a persistent\n   knowledge base for troubleshooting.\n2. Create one file for each MCP test session. Name the file\n   `mcp-{server}-{YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM}.md`.\n3. Record the date, server, tool, request, response summary, expected result,\n   actual result, cause, and fix in each file.\n4. Redact secrets and personal data before you write the file.\n5. Keep each result file that supports a recorded fix. Do not delete it.\n6. Change the storage folder only when the owner asks. Edit this subsection, then\n   move the existing result files to the new folder.\n\n## 11. Autonomy and safety\n\nAuto-approved:\n\n- reads, symbol lookup, documentation queries,\n- read-only state and store queries,\n- log, console, and network inspection with bounded output,\n- task-scoped source edits,\n- focused tests, read-only projections, and syntax checks,\n- read-only git commands.\n\nExplicit confirmation required:\n\n- destructive data mutation, migration, `DROP`, or `TRUNCATE`,\n- force-push, history rewrite, branch deletion, or remote change,\n- production write, release, deploy, or billing action,\n- secret, credential, token, vault, or environment file change,\n- new runtime dependency,\n- build or installer creation,\n- global tooling change,\n- deletion or move of source, test, or user data outside the task.\n\nNever:\n\n- commit a secret or personal data,\n- stage or commit a file that contains a likely secret,\n- print, quote, or copy a secret into chat, logs, commands, or generated files,\n- write project files outside the project root,\n- store project data outside the project root at any time,\n- store a database or data file outside the `data\\` subfolder,\n- modify or remove a file or folder outside the project root during analysis,\n- weaken a gate to make a build pass,\n- claim a check that did not run,\n- hide `.git`,\n- write to production or a remote environment without instruction.\n\n## 12. Definition of done\n\nA change is complete only when every applicable item is true.\n\n- The requested behavior works.\n- The smallest correct cause was changed.\n- No unrequested setting or surface was added.\n- Focused verification passed.\n- Full verification passed, or the gap is stated.\n- Gates passed, or the baseline rule is stated.\n- Documentation matches code.\n- Roadmap facts were re-measured.\n- User data and secrets are untouched or safely migrated.\n- `git status --short`, `git diff`, and `git log -1 --oneline` were reviewed.\n- The commit is on `main` with a message that states what and why.\n- No generated artifact was created without permission.\n- The final report names the checks that ran and the checks that did not.\n",
  "SKILLS.md": "# SKILLS.md\n\nUse these skills when work repeats across projects. Keep rules in `AGENTS.md`.\nKeep product behavior in `README.md`. Keep measured status in `ROADMAP.md`.\n\n> This template is designed for use with agentic coding agents (e.g., Freebuff, Opencode, Qoder, GitHub Copilot, Kilo Code, Kiro, Cursor, and similar tools). It provides reusable procedures that agents can load and execute to maintain consistency across the project.\n\nThe open Agent Skills standard at [agentskills.io](https://agentskills.io/)\ndefines `SKILL.md` as a portable folder of agent instructions. It packages\nspecialized knowledge and repeatable workflows. Agents load a skill when a task\nmatches its description. This file keeps those procedures in one catalogue.\n\n## Catalogue\n\n| Section | Skill | Use when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | Start a new project | A project has no onboarding record. |\n| 2 | Audit an existing project | An existing project needs a documentation baseline. |\n| 3 | Compare documents with code | A rule may disagree with executable configuration. |\n| 4 | Write an `AGENTS.md` | The project needs a rulebook. |\n| 5 | Write a `README.md` | The project needs an operator guide. |\n| 6 | Write a `ROADMAP.md` | The project needs measured status and open work. |\n| 7 | Engineer a regression gate | A defect needs a guard that cannot silently pass. |\n| 8 | Prepare a build gate | An artifact requires checks before creation. |\n| 9 | Design a build system | The project needs a supported artifact path. |\n| 10 | Write a `DESIGN.md` | The project needs UI components, tokens, and accessibility rules. |\n| 11 | Analyze local sources | A template needs evidence from real projects. |\n| 12 | Write controlled prose | Docs, comments, or commit text need review. |\n| 13 | Resolve a conflict | Two documents or settings make different claims. |\n| 14 | Apply a change | A defect or request needs a controlled edit. |\n| 15 | Finish a change | Work needs verification and a final report. |\n| 16 | Run an onboarding interview | Required project facts are missing. |\n| 17 | Resolve contradictions | Documents disagree with executable configuration. |\n| 18 | Document MCP integration | The project exposes or consumes MCP surfaces. |\n| 19 | Update an existing project | A project needs its docs brought to the current template. |\n\n## 1. Start a new project\n\n1. Run the onboarding interview in section 16.\n2. Copy `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, `ROADMAP.md`, and this file into the project root.\n3. Create the `data\\` subfolder for databases and data files.\n4. Replace every placeholder token with a verified answer.\n5. Record the project root, owner, runtime, commands, and output path in `AGENTS.md`.\n6. Record the product purpose and setup steps in `README.md`.\n7. Record the first measured status entry in `ROADMAP.md`.\n8. Run the local gates before the first edit.\n\nUse section 19 for a project that already has these documents.\n\n## 2. Audit an existing project\n\n1. List the root files and executable configuration.\n2. Read each current document that owns project facts.\n3. Identify missing rules, stale commands, and unsupported claims.\n4. Run the contradiction check when one exists.\n5. Name each conflict in one sentence.\n6. Ask the owner which source is true when code alone cannot decide.\n7. Repair the losing document.\n8. Record each decision and date in `ROADMAP.md`.\n\nUse section 19 to apply the current template after the audit.\n\n## 3. Compare documents with code\n\n1. Select one documented command or rule.\n2. Find the executable source that governs it.\n3. Run the command or inspect the configuration.\n4. Record the observed result.\n5. Trust executable configuration until the owner decides otherwise.\n6. Repair the losing document.\n7. Repeat for the next documented command or rule.\n\n## 4. Write an `AGENTS.md`\n\n1. Use one H1 with the filename.\n2. State the project root, owner, default branch, and review date.\n3. Own stack facts, commands, rules, and file ownership in this file.\n4. Include onboarding questions and the contradiction protocol.\n5. Include writing, engineering, testing, Git, build, MCP, and safety rules.\n6. Link to focused subdocuments instead of copying their content.\n7. Verify every command against executable configuration.\n\n## 5. Write a `README.md`\n\n1. Use one H1 with the product title.\n2. State the product purpose in one sentence.\n3. Add a short status callout when work is incomplete.\n4. Own setup, controls, behavior, procedures, and terminology in this file.\n5. Add a document map that links to `AGENTS.md` and `ROADMAP.md`.\n6. State known limits and missing verification.\n7. Keep historical notes in dated sections.\n\n## 6. Write a `ROADMAP.md`\n\n1. Use one H1 with the project title and `Roadmap`.\n2. State that `AGENTS.md` owns rules and `README.md` owns product behavior.\n3. Record only measured status.\n4. Give each open item a closure check.\n5. Record decisions with a date and the winning source.\n6. Record shipped work in a change log.\n7. Re-measure every figure before editing it.\n\n## 7. Engineer a regression gate\n\n1. Reproduce the defect with a named command or surface.\n2. Capture the actual failing output.\n3. State one cause in one sentence.\n4. Write a check that fails on that cause.\n5. Keep the check deterministic and bounded.\n6. Print `PASS: ...` or `FAIL: ...`.\n7. Exit with `0` on pass and non-zero on failure.\n8. Store the gate in `build\\gates\\` under version control.\n9. Document the gate in the owning subsystem guide.\n\n## 8. Prepare a build gate\n\n1. Run all hard gates before an artifact is written.\n2. Stop the build when any hard gate fails.\n3. Do not skip, weaken, or suppress a gate.\n4. State the artifact name and output folder.\n5. Record the commit, branch, uncommitted paths, and gate verdict.\n6. State any check that did not run.\n7. Ask for permission before a new build when no standing approval exists.\n\n## 9. Design a build system\n\n1. Name one supported build entry point.\n2. Resolve paths at runtime.\n3. Write artifacts only to the documented output directory.\n4. Keep one artifact shape.\n5. Keep staging inside the checkout.\n6. Clean staging after success and failure.\n7. Rebuild the build tool after its source changes.\n8. Verify the artifact with the documented smoke test.\n9. Keep `favicon.ico` at 16x16 pixels. A larger icon adds bytes without\n   improving the browser tab.\n\n## 10. Write a `DESIGN.md`\n\n1. List approved UI components with markup and CSS examples.\n2. Define design tokens: colors, spacing, typography, radius, shadows.\n3. Specify interaction requirements: focus, touch, animations.\n4. Specify accessibility requirements: semantic HTML, ARIA, contrast, text.\n5. List explicit prohibitions: obsolete HTML, CSS, and JavaScript APIs.\n6. Add platform-specific sections (Windows desktop, web app).\n7. Document linting and enforcement rules.\n8. Provide a verification checklist for PR review.\n9. Link `DESIGN.md` from `AGENTS.md` authority section.\n\n## 11. Analyze local sources\n\n1. List candidate projects before reading files.\n2. Exclude dependencies, generated output, binaries, vendor files, and private notes.\n3. Record each source path and the files read.\n4. Count repeated patterns across sources.\n5. Separate universal patterns from project-specific patterns.\n6. Record conflicts between sources.\n7. Turn only evidence-backed patterns into templates.\n\n## 12. Write controlled prose\n\n1. Use short words and one meaning per word.\n2. Use the same term for the same thing every time.\n3. Write instructions in imperative form.\n4. Put a condition before the instruction it governs.\n5. Keep an instruction sentence to 20 words or fewer.\n6. Keep a descriptive sentence to 25 words or fewer.\n7. Do not use semicolons, contractions, or em dashes.\n8. Wrap paths, commands, and literal values in backticks.\n9. Use a vertical list for a complex sequence.\n\n## 13. Resolve a conflict\n\n1. Name one conflict in one sentence.\n2. Name the two sources.\n3. Identify the executable source when one exists.\n4. Trust executable configuration until the owner answers.\n5. Ask the owner when the true source is unclear.\n6. Repair the losing source.\n7. Record the decision and date in `ROADMAP.md`.\n8. Repeat for the next conflict.\n\n## 14. Apply a change\n\n1. Reproduce the failure.\n2. Inspect bounded logs, state, and source.\n3. State one cause in one sentence.\n4. Change the smallest correct cause.\n5. Do not weaken an assertion or skip a gate.\n6. Run the focused check.\n7. Run the broader check when shared code changed.\n8. Repeat the original reproduction.\n9. Update the owning document.\n\n## 15. Finish a change\n\n1. Confirm the requested behavior works.\n2. Confirm no unrequested surface was added.\n3. Confirm focused and full verification results.\n4. Confirm gate verdicts.\n5. Re-measure roadmap figures.\n6. Confirm secrets and user data are untouched or safely migrated.\n7. Review status, diff, and recent history when Git is present.\n8. State which checks ran and which checks did not run.\n\n## 16. Run an onboarding interview\n\nAsk one question at a time. Use the required questions in `AGENTS.md` section 0.\nWait for each answer before asking the next question. Offer one short answer\nfield. Offer a default when a default is known. Do not combine questions. Do not\ninfer a missing answer from a path.\n\nUse these defaults. For Q2 offer the current working directory and allow a\ncustom directory. For Q4 default to `main` and allow an override. For Q6 offer a\ntier list of the best runtime and stack choices with a short reason for each and\nallow a custom entry. For Q7 use plain words such as which command should create\nyour final product or installer. For Q8 default to `check` and allow an\nalternative. For Q9 default to `builds\\` and name installers\n`{ProjectName}-Setup-YYYY.MM.DD-HHMM-x64.exe` where `HHMM` is the local build\ntime.\nFor Q10 offer the best Model Context Protocol (MCP) surface for the project and\nexplain what it does. For Q11 bring each contradiction to the user and ask the\nuser to resolve it.\n\nRecord each answer in the owning document. Ask a follow-up question when an\nanswer is unclear. Stop when every required answer is known.\n\nUse a local onboarding runner when one exists. Use a copyable prompt when one\nexists. Store answers in `onboarding-answers.json`.\n\n## 17. Resolve contradictions\n\nRun the local contradiction checker before manual review when one exists.\nCompare each current document with executable configuration. Name one conflict\nin one sentence. Ask which source is true. Trust executable configuration until\nthe owner answers.\n\nRepair the losing document. Record the decision and date in `ROADMAP.md`.\nRepeat from the next conflict. Record a missing checker as a gap. Do not\nsimulate its output.\n\n## 18. Document MCP integration\n\nRun local MCP discovery when one exists. Record the project MCP role, server\npackages, configuration path, exposed tools, and trust boundary in `AGENTS.md`\nsection 10.\n\nTreat MCP tool input as untrusted. Validate every tool argument against its\nschema. Put write tools behind explicit confirmation. Redact secrets in logs and\nerror reports.\n\nKeep credentials in the environment or an untracked local file. Restart a\nclient after its MCP configuration changes. Document each tool in `README.md`\nor a subsystem document. Record a missing tool as a gap. Do not simulate its\noutput.\n\nStore every MCP test result in the `tests\\` folder of the project root. Section\n10.1 of `AGENTS.md` owns the folder, the file name, and the record fields.\n\n## 19. Update an existing project\n\nUse this skill to bring an existing project onto the current template.\n\n1. Run the audit in section 2. List the stale and missing sections.\n2. Read the stored answers. Mark each answer that code or config contradicts.\n3. Ask the owner for each marked answer. Ask one question at a time.\n4. Apply the current template sections to the project documents.\n5. Keep every verified project fact. Replace only stale or missing content.\n6. Preserve the decisions and dated history in `ROADMAP.md`.\n7. Record the update with the date, source, change, and gate result.\n8. Run the checks in section 15. State each check that did not run.\n\nClosure check: no placeholder remains, every answer matches configuration, and\nall gates pass.\n",
  "README.md": "# {{PROJECT_TITLE}}\n\n{{PRODUCT_PURPOSE}}\n\n> Status: `{{STATUS_SUMMARY}}`. See `ROADMAP.md` for measured status and open work.\n>\n> This template is designed for use with agentic coding agents (e.g., Freebuff, Opencode, Qoder, GitHub Copilot, Kilo Code, Kiro, Cursor, and similar tools). It provides a structured product document that agents can read to understand the project's behavior, setup, and procedures.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis document owns product behavior, setup, controls, operator procedures, and\nterminology. `AGENTS.md` owns rules and commands. `ROADMAP.md` owns measured\nstatus, decisions, and change history. `DESIGN.md` owns UI components, tokens,\ninteraction, accessibility, and prohibitions.\n\nLink to a focused subdocument instead of copying its content.\n\nData storage rules live in `AGENTS.md` section 1 and section 5.\n\n## Document map\n\n| Document | Use |\n| --- | --- |\n| `AGENTS.md` | Rules, stack facts, commands, and gates. |\n| `SKILLS.md` | Repeatable work procedures. |\n| `README.md` | Product behavior and operator procedures. |\n| `ROADMAP.md` | Status, open work, decisions, and change history. |\n| `DESIGN.md` | UI components, tokens, interaction, accessibility, prohibitions. |\n\n## Setup\n\n1. Verify the required runtime: `{{RUNTIME}}`.\n2. Install dependencies with `{{INSTALL_COMMAND}}`.\n3. Configure required values with `{{CONFIGURATION_STEPS}}`.\n4. Confirm the setup with `{{VERIFY_SETUP_COMMAND}}`.\n\n## Update an existing project\n\nUse this section to refresh an already onboarded project.\n\n1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `SKILLS.md`, `README.md`, `ROADMAP.md`, and `DESIGN.md`.\n2. Run the local checks. Record the current result.\n3. Compare each documented command with executable configuration.\n4. Confirm missing or contradicted answers with the owner. Ask one question at\n   a time.\n5. Apply the current template sections. Keep every verified project fact.\n6. Preserve decisions and dated history in `ROADMAP.md`.\n7. Run the checks in the Verification section. Record the result and each\n   skipped check.\n\nDo not replace a verified fact with a placeholder. Do not remove a dated record.\n\n## Operation\n\n| Task | Command or control | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Start | `{{START_COMMAND}}` | `{{START_RESULT}}` |\n| Use primary control | `{{PRIMARY_CONTROL}}` | `{{PRIMARY_RESULT}}` |\n| Stop | `{{STOP_COMMAND}}` | `{{STOP_RESULT}}` |\n\nAdd one row for each operator task. State a condition before the instruction it\ngoverns. Mark a safety statement with `WARNING`, `CAUTION`, or `Note`.\n\n## Verification\n\n1. Run the focused check with `{{FOCUSED_CHECK_COMMAND}}`.\n2. Run the gates with `{{GATE_COMMAND}}`.\n3. Run the full check with `{{FULL_CHECK_COMMAND}}`.\n4. Run the smoke test with `{{SMOKE_TEST_COMMAND}}` when available.\n5. State any check that did not run.\n\nA missing tool is a failure or a stated gap. It is never a silent pass.\n\n## MCP surface\n\n| Item | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project MCP role | `{{MCP_ROLE}}` |\n| Server packages | `{{MCP_SERVER_PACKAGES}}` |\n| Configuration path | `{{MCP_CONFIG_PATH}}` |\n| Exposed tools | `{{MCP_TOOLS}}` |\n| Trust boundary | `{{MCP_TRUST_BOUNDARY}}` |\n\nDocument each exposed tool here or in a focused subdocument. Put write tools\nbehind explicit confirmation. Redact secrets in logs and error reports.\n\nStore every MCP test result in the `tests\\` folder of the project root. The\nstored results form a persistent knowledge base for troubleshooting. `AGENTS.md`\nsection 10.1 owns the folder, the file name, and the record fields.\n\nChange the storage folder only when the owner asks. Edit `AGENTS.md` section\n10.1, then move the existing result files to the new folder.\n\n## Terminology\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `{{TERM}}` | `{{TERM_MEANING}}` |\n\nUse one meaning for each term. Use the same term for the same thing every time.\n\n## Known limits\n\n- `{{LIMIT}}`\n\nState each limit that affects an operator. Do not hide a missing check.\n",
  "ROADMAP.md": "# {{PROJECT_TITLE}} Roadmap\n\n`AGENTS.md` owns rules and commands. `README.md` owns product behavior and\noperator procedures. `DESIGN.md` owns UI components, tokens, interaction,\naccessibility, and prohibitions. This file owns measured status, open work,\ndecisions, and change history.\n\n> This template is designed for use with agentic coding agents (e.g., Freebuff, Opencode, Qoder, GitHub Copilot, Kilo Code, Kiro, Cursor, and similar tools). It provides a structured roadmap that agents can read and update to track project status, open work, and decisions.\n\n## Status snapshot\n\n| Area | Status | Evidence | Last measured |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `{{AREA}}` | `{{STATUS}}` | `{{EVIDENCE}}` | `{{DATE}}` |\n\nUse verified evidence only. Re-measure every figure before editing it.\n\n## Open work\n\n| ID | Item | Closure check | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `{{ITEM_ID}}` | `{{ITEM}}` | `{{CLOSURE_CHECK}}` | `{{OWNER}}` |\n\nAdd no speculative item. Every item states a closure check. Move shipped work\nout of this table. Do not duplicate it.\n\n## Decisions\n\n| Date | Decision | Winning source | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `{{DATE}}` | `{{DECISION}}` | `{{SOURCE}}` | `{{REASON}}` |\n\nTrust executable configuration until the owner decides otherwise. Record each\ncontradiction decision here. Keep historical decisions dated.\n\n## Update record\n\nRecord each refresh of this project with the template source and gate result.\n\n| Date | Template source | Change | Gate result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `{{DATE}}` | `{{TEMPLATE_SOURCE}}` | `{{UPDATE_CHANGE}}` | `{{GATE_RESULT}}` |\n\nUse a verified template version or source commit. Keep each entry dated. Do not\nrewrite an earlier entry.\n\n## Change log\n\n| Date | Change | Verification | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `{{DATE}}` | `{{CHANGE}}` | `{{VERIFICATION}}` | `{{RESULT}}` |\n\nRecord a build, measurement, or tooling investigation with the fix and the rule\nthat must not break.\n",
  "DESIGN.md": "# DESIGN.md\n\nDesign rules for `{{PROJECT_TITLE}}` UI surfaces.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis document records approved UI components, design tokens, interaction requirements, accessibility rules, and explicit prohibitions. Agents must read this guide before changing any UI surface.\n\n## 2. Scope\n\n- Applies to all HTML, CSS, and JavaScript in this project.\n- Applies to template source and generated artifacts.\n- Does not apply to external documentation rendered by the project.\n\n> Replace every `{{PLACEHOLDER}}` before using this file. This template provides a starting point. Adapt tokens, components, and rules to the project's actual design system.\n\n## 3. Design tokens\n\n### 3.1 Colors\n\n| Token | Light | Dark | Use |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `--bg` | `#ffffff` | `#0f0f0f` | Page background |\n| `--panel` | `#f5f5f5` | `#181818` | Card/panel background |\n| `--panel-2` | `#eeeeee` | `#202020` | Secondary panel |\n| `--text` | `#1a1a1a` | `#f3f4f6` | Primary text |\n| `--muted` | `#6b6b6b` | `#a3a3a3` | Secondary text |\n| `--accent` | `#2563eb` | `#60a5fa` | Primary action, focus ring |\n| `--border` | `#d1d5db` | `#2a2a2a` | Borders, dividers |\n| `--success` | `#059669` | `#34d399` | Success state |\n| `--warning` | `#d97706` | `#fbbf24` | Warning state |\n| `--destructive` | `#dc2626` | `#ef4444` | Error, destructive action |\n\n### 3.2 Spacing\n\n| Token | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--space-1` | 4px |\n| `--space-2` | 8px |\n| `--space-3` | 12px |\n| `--space-4` | 16px |\n| `--space-5` | 24px |\n| `--space-6` | 32px |\n\n### 3.3 Typography\n\n| Token | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--font-sans` | `Poppins, system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif` |\n| `--font-mono` | `ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace` |\n| `--text-xs` | 12px |\n| `--text-sm` | 14px |\n| `--text-base` | 16px |\n| `--text-lg` | 18px |\n| `--text-xl` | 20px |\n\n- All written content, form elements, buttons, and other GUI elements must use an embedded Poppins font as the default font face and 16px as the default font size.\n- Poppins is embedded locally with `font-display: swap`.\n- Settings must allow the user to change the font face and font size.\n\n### 3.4 Radius and shadows\n\n| Token | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--radius-sm` | 4px |\n| `--radius` | 8px |\n| `--radius-lg` | 12px |\n| `--shadow-sm` | `0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)` |\n| `--shadow` | `0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)` |\n| `--shadow-lg` | `0 10px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.1)` |\n\n## 4. Approved components\n\n### 4.1 Buttons\n\n```html\n<button class=\"btn btn-primary\">Primary</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-secondary\">Secondary</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-ghost\">Ghost</button>\n<button class=\"btn btn-destructive\">Destructive</button>\n```\n\n- Primary: `--accent` background, white text, `--radius`\n- Secondary: `--panel-2` background, `--text` text, `--border` border\n- Ghost: transparent background, `--text` text, hover shows `--panel-2`\n- Destructive: `--destructive` background, white text\n\nAll buttons:\n- `min-height: 36px`\n- `padding: 0 16px`\n- `font: inherit`\n- `cursor: pointer`\n- `:focus-visible` shows 2px `--accent` outline with 2px offset\n- `:disabled` opacity 0.5, `cursor: not-allowed`\n\n### 4.2 Inputs\n\n```html\n<input type=\"text\" class=\"input\" placeholder=\"Enter value\" />\n<select class=\"input\">...</select>\n<textarea class=\"input\">...</textarea>\n```\n\n- `--panel` background, `--border` border, `--radius`\n- `padding: 8px 12px`\n- `font: inherit`\n- `:focus` border `--accent`, no outline\n- `:disabled` opacity 0.5\n\n### 4.3 Tabs\n\n```html\n<nav class=\"tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Sections\">\n  <button role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\">Tab 1</button>\n  <button role=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"false\" tabindex=\"-1\">Tab 2</button>\n</nav>\n<div role=\"tabpanel\" aria-labelledby=\"tab-1\" tabindex=\"0\">...</div>\n```\n\n- Horizontal layout, gap 2px\n- Active tab: `--bg` background, `--border` border, bottom border `--bg`\n- Inactive tab: `--panel-2` background\n- `:focus-visible` outline 2px `--accent`\n- Keyboard: Arrow keys navigate, Home/End jump, Enter/Space activate\n\n### 4.4 Cards\n\n```html\n<article class=\"card\">\n  <img class=\"card-img\" src=\"...\" alt=\"\" />\n  <div class=\"card-body\">\n    <h3 class=\"card-title\">Title</h3>\n    <p class=\"card-text\">Description</p>\n  </div>\n</article>\n```\n\n- `--panel` background with backdrop blur\n- `--border` border (10% opacity light / 10% white dark)\n- `--radius-lg` radius\n- `overflow: hidden`\n- Hover: slight lift, border accent color\n\n### 4.5 Modal / Dialog\n\n```html\n<div class=\"modal\" role=\"dialog\" aria-modal=\"true\" aria-labelledby=\"modal-title\">\n  <div class=\"modal-content\">\n    <header class=\"modal-header\">\n      <h2 id=\"modal-title\">Title</h2>\n      <button class=\"modal-close\" aria-label=\"Close\">×</button>\n    </header>\n    <div class=\"modal-body\">...</div>\n    <footer class=\"modal-footer\">\n      <button class=\"btn btn-secondary\">Cancel</button>\n      <button class=\"btn btn-primary\">Confirm</button>\n    </footer>\n  </div>\n</div>\n```\n\n- Fixed overlay, centered\n- Backdrop: `rgba(0,0,0,0.55)`\n- `--panel` background, `--radius-lg`\n- Trap focus inside while open\n- Escape key closes\n\n### 4.6 Tooltips\n\n```html\n<button class=\"has-tooltip\" data-tooltip=\"Tooltip text\">Hover me</button>\n```\n\n- `--text` text on `--bg` background\n- `--radius-sm`, padding 6px 10px\n- Small arrow pointing to trigger\n- Show on hover/focus, hide on leave/blur\n\n## 5. Interaction requirements\n\n### 5.1 Focus management\n\n- Every interactive element must be keyboard reachable\n- Focus order matches visual order\n- Focus visible state uses 2px `--accent` outline with 2px offset\n- Never remove focus outline without replacement\n- Modal dialogs trap focus\n- Skip links provided for main content areas\n\n### 5.2 Touch and pointer\n\n- Minimum touch target 44x44px\n- Hover states not required for function\n- Active/tap state provides immediate feedback\n- Drag operations have keyboard alternative\n\n### 5.3 Animations\n\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`\n- Default transitions: 150-250ms ease\n- No auto-playing animations > 5s without pause control\n\n### 5.4 Presenting multiple options\n\n- When presenting multiple options, never use a bulleted or numbered list.\n- Render each option as a selectable card in a responsive grid.\n- Keep each card concise.\n- Make the whole card selectable.\n- Let the grid wrap to fit the available screen width and height.\n- If the interface cannot render interactive cards, say so rather than pretending plain-text boxes are selectable.\n- If an agentic agent or CLI cannot render selectable cards, use a grid picker instead.\n\n## 6. Accessibility requirements\n\n### 6.1 Semantic HTML\n\n- Use native HTML elements over custom ARIA when possible\n- `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation\n- Heading hierarchy: one `<h1>`, sequential `<h2>`-`<h6>`\n- Lists use `<ul>`/`<ol>`/`<li>`\n- Forms use `<label>` with `for`/`id` or wrapping\n\n### 6.2 ARIA\n\n- `role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, `role=\"tabpanel\"` for tabs\n- `role=\"dialog\"`, `aria-modal=\"true\"` for modals\n- `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` for icon-only buttons\n- `aria-expanded`/`aria-controls` for disclosures\n- `aria-live=\"polite\"` for status updates\n- Do not use `role=\"button\"` on non-interactive elements\n\n### 6.3 Color and contrast\n\n- Minimum 4.5:1 contrast for normal text (WCAG AA)\n- Minimum 3:1 contrast for large text (18pt+/14pt+ bold)\n- Minimum 3:1 for UI components and graphics\n- Do not convey information by color alone\n- Test in both light and dark modes\n\n### 6.4 Text and content\n\n- No horizontal scrolling at 320px width\n- Text resize to 200% without loss of function\n- Line height ≥ 1.5, paragraph spacing ≥ 2x line height\n- Letter spacing ≥ 0.12em, word spacing ≥ 0.16em\n\n## 7. Explicit prohibitions\n\n### 7.1 HTML elements to avoid\n\nDo not use these obsolete or non-standard elements in new code:\n\n| Element | Replacement |\n| --- | --- |\n| `<acronym>` | `<abbr>` |\n| `<big>` | CSS `font-size` |\n| `<center>` | CSS `text-align: center` |\n| `<font>` | CSS `font-family`, `color`, `font-size` |\n| `<marquee>` | CSS animation |\n| `<strike>` | `<del>` or `<s>` |\n| `<tt>` | `<code>`, `<kbd>`, `<samp>`, or CSS `font-family: monospace` |\n| `<frame>` | `<iframe>` or server-side includes |\n| `<frameset>` | CSS layout |\n| `<noframes>` | Remove |\n| `<noembed>` | Remove |\n| `<param>` | Remove (used only in `<object>`) |\n| `<plaintext>` | `<pre>` or `<code>` |\n| `<content>` | `<slot>` (Shadow DOM) |\n| `<shadow>` | `<slot>` (Shadow DOM) |\n| `<menuitem>` | `<button>` in `<menu>` or custom component |\n\n### 7.2 CSS properties to avoid\n\n- Do not use obsolete properties as defaults (e.g., `clip` → use `clip-path`)\n- Do not use vendor-prefixed properties (`-webkit-`, `-moz-`, `-ms-`) unless a documented browser-support requirement calls for them\n- Check MDN and caniuse.com before adding any property with questionable support\n- Do not use non-standard properties as primary styling\n\n### 7.3 JavaScript APIs to avoid\n\nDo not use deprecated legacy APIs:\n\n| Deprecated API | Replacement |\n| --- | --- |\n| `escape()` / `unescape()` | `encodeURIComponent()` / `decodeURIComponent()` |\n| `RegExp.prototype.compile()` | `new RegExp()` |\n| `Object.prototype.__defineGetter__()` | `Object.defineProperty()` |\n| `Object.prototype.__defineSetter__()` | `Object.defineProperty()` |\n| `String.prototype.trimLeft()` | `String.prototype.trimStart()` |\n| `String.prototype.trimRight()` | `String.prototype.trimEnd()` |\n| `document.all` | `document.getElementById()`, `querySelector()` |\n| `document.layers` | `document.querySelectorAll()` |\n| `navigator.userAgent` for feature detection | Feature detection (`'feature' in element`) |\n\n### 7.4 General prohibitions\n\n- No inline styles (`style=\"...\"`) except for dynamic values set by JavaScript\n- No `!important` except for utility classes explicitly documented as overrides\n- No fixed pixel widths on fluid containers\n- No `overflow: hidden` on `<body>` or `<html>` that prevents scrolling\n- No removal of native focus styles without accessible replacement\n- No keyboard traps\n- No autoplaying audio\n- No content that flashes > 3 times per second\n\n## 8. Windows desktop app specific rules\n\n### 8.1 Window chrome\n\n- Custom titlebar: three square buttons (minimize, maximize/restore, close) in top-right\n- `-webkit-app-region: drag` on draggable areas\n- `-webkit-app-region: no-drag` on interactive elements\n- Minimum window size enforced\n\n### 8.2 System integration\n\n- Respect system theme preference (`prefers-color-scheme`)\n- Respect system accent color where applicable\n- High contrast mode support\n- DPI scaling: test at 100%, 125%, 150%, 200%\n\n### 8.3 File handling\n\n- Drag-and-drop from Explorer supported\n- File picker uses native dialog\n- Context menu integration where applicable\n\n## 9. Web app specific rules\n\n### 9.1 Responsive breakpoints\n\n| Breakpoint | Width | Columns |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Mobile | ≤ 600px | 2 |\n| Tablet | 601-900px | 4 |\n| Desktop | 901-1200px | 5 |\n| Wide | 1201-1400px | 6 |\n| Ultra | > 1400px | auto-fill |\n\n### 9.2 PWA considerations\n\n- Service worker for offline shell\n- Manifest with icons, theme color, display mode\n- Install prompt handled gracefully\n\n### 9.3 Performance\n\n- Critical CSS inlined\n- Fonts: `font-display: swap`\n- Images: lazy loading, WebP with fallback\n- JavaScript: code splitting, defer non-critical\n\n## 10. Linting and enforcement\n\n### 10.1 HTML\n\n- `html-validate` or equivalent for semantic checks\n- Check for prohibited elements in CI/gate\n\n### 10.2 CSS\n\n- `stylelint` with recommended config\n- Custom rule: flag prohibited properties\n- Custom rule: flag vendor prefixes without fallback\n\n### 10.3 JavaScript\n\n- ESLint with `eslint-plugin-compat` for browser compat\n- Custom rule: flag deprecated APIs\n- TypeScript `noImplicitAny`, `strictNullChecks`\n\n## 11. Verification checklist\n\nBefore merging UI changes, verify:\n\n- [ ] All interactive elements keyboard accessible\n- [ ] Focus visible on all interactive elements\n- [ ] Color contrast meets WCAG AA in both themes\n- [ ] No prohibited HTML elements\n- [ ] No prohibited CSS properties\n- [ ] No prohibited JavaScript APIs\n- [ ] Responsive at all breakpoints\n- [ ] `prefers-reduced-motion` respected\n- [ ] High contrast mode functional\n- [ ] Screen reader announces dynamic changes\n- [ ] No horizontal scroll at 320px\n- [ ] Text resizes to 200% without loss\n\n## 12. References\n\n- [MDN Web Docs: HTML elements](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element)\n- [MDN Web Docs: CSS Reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference)\n- [MDN Web Docs: JavaScript Reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference)\n- [WCAG 2.1 Guidelines](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/quickref/)\n- [WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/)\n- [can i use](https://caniuse.com/)"
};
